     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Albanese Omits Fossil Fuels in Cop31 Speech Amid Criticism and Guterres's 'No New Coal' Call</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Australia</span></div>
       <p>Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese addressed the UN’s climate summit, Cop31, in Antalya, Turkey, on Wednesday morning, emphasizing governments’ “solemn responsibility” to act on climate change. However, his speech notably failed to mention the fossil fuels driving the crisis, drawing criticism despite his boasts of Australia's success in renewables. The talks are being presided over by Australia’s climate change minister, Chris Bowen, and hosted by Turkey.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/albanese-omits-fossil-fuels-in-cop31-speech-amid-criticism-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Moments before Albanese spoke, UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres explicitly stated on the same stage that keeping the 1.5C temperature goal alive “means no new coal.” Albanese, however, highlighted Australia's &quot;significant successes&quot; in climate action, including doubling renewable energy generation in six years, which now accounts for half of the country’s electricity. He also pointed to Australia's position as a leader in rooftop solar and battery markets.</p><p>Despite these claims, Australia is the world’s second-largest exporter of coal for power stations and the largest exporter of coal for steelmaking. A fifth of the world’s exported LNG also originates from Australia. Guterres has consistently called for detailed national roadmaps to transition away from fossil fuels, urging coal power to fall by at least 75% by 2035, unabated coal phased out by 2040, and oil and gas use cut by a third.</p><p>Adam Bandt of the Australian Conservation Foundation called Albanese’s speech a “snub” to the secretary-general, citing the prime minister's refusal to address coal and gas expansion. Bill Hare of Climate Analytics found it “disturbing” that fossil fuels were not mentioned, noting that 38 fossil projects have been approved by the Albanese government since 2022. Australia and Turkey are seeking support for a global goal to see 35% of final-use energy from electricity by 2035, up from about a fifth today. Chris Bowen questioned whether multilateralism could rise to the “polycrisis” of climate change, energy security, conflict, and AI power demands, stating, &quot;This fortnight we can answer those legitimate concerns and questions with a simple answer: Yes.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing Cop31 talks for progress on fossil fuel transition plans.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article provides a comprehensive summary of the event, incorporating all key figures, quotes, and the specific context provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
