     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bengaluru Parents Turn Advocates After Daughter's Medical Ordeal</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Positive News</span> <span>#Healthcare</span> <span>#Parenting</span></div>
       <p>Gouthami Ravi and Sharan Kulkarni of Bengaluru are now advocates for other parents after their daughter, Chikoo, overcame a challenging premature birth and months of intensive medical care. Chikoo, born seven weeks premature on 2 September 2025, weighed just 2.05 kg and required immediate breathing support in a neonatal intensive care unit (NICU). After an initial discharge, a month into her life, she experienced frightening choking episodes that led to falling oxygen levels and sustained respiratory support.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/bengaluru-parents-turn-advocates-after-daughters-medical-ord.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Further investigations revealed a combination of airway conditions, necessitating a tracheostomy, a procedure performed in October 2025 after an initial unsuccessful attempt. For months, Chikoo's tracheostomy tube prevented her parents from hearing her cry, turning their home into a medical care unit. Sharan maintained a steadfast belief in her survival, stating, &quot;It was always 100%. She's going to survive,&quot; which sustained the family through numerous hospital admissions and medical decisions.</p><p>The financial strain was considerable, with medical bills reaching approximately Rs 35 lakh, only partially covered by insurance. The parents underwent 20 days of practical training to manage Chikoo's tracheostomy care at home, learning procedures, equipment, and emergency responses. Gouthami credits her employer, SAP, for crucial medical insurance and mental health support, while Sharan reorganized his business to work from home, with both relying heavily on family support.</p><p>On 10 March 2026, after careful trials, Chikoo's tracheostomy was permanently removed. Sharan recalls the emotional moment when they heard her cry during a cleaning, a sound that brought tears to the family and nurses. Today, Chikoo is a curious, energetic one-year-old who attends daycare, and while a few developmental milestones are still catching up, she is full of life. Gouthami and Sharan now aim to support other families facing similar journeys, offering practical guidance on medical questions, home preparation, and managing the emotional and financial demands of long-term care, ensuring no parent feels as alone as they once did.</p><p>Why it matters: Sharing personal journeys provides crucial support and practical guidance for families navigating complex medical challenges.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively summarizes the emotional and practical journey of the family, covering the medical, financial, and personal aspects provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/parenting/baby-tracheostomy-care-parenting-journey-bengaluru-12801460" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/bengaluru/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bengaluru</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
