     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bennu Asteroid and Murchison Meteorite Amino Acids Hint at Diverse Cosmic Origins</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Asteroid</span> <span>#Bennu</span> <span>#Amino Acids</span></div>
       <p>New research analyzing samples from the Bennu asteroid, currently over 200 million miles from Earth, and the Murchison meteorite, which fell in 1969, reveals that molecules essential for life, known as amino acids, may have formed through similar processes but originated in vastly different parts of the solar system. This finding challenges prevailing ideas about the formation environments of life's building blocks, according to Phys.org.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/bennu-asteroid-and-murchison-meteorite-amino-acids-hint-at-d.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers, including a team from Penn State, employed a novel technique to study isotopes—slight variations in atomic mass—within amino acids from a Bennu sample delivered by NASA's OSIRIS-REx mission in 2023, and from Murchison. The direct-from-space Bennu sample provided uncontaminated insights into these molecules' formation locations. The wide range of isotope values measured within the amino acids suggested that their carbon and nitrogen atoms originated in disparate solar system regions, findings published in Communications Earth &amp; Environment.</p><p>Ophélie Mcintosh, lead author and former Penn State postdoctoral researcher, highlighted that amino acids, found in carbon-rich meteorites and asteroid samples, offer a direct link between space chemistry and life's origins on Earth. While most studies focus on distribution, this research delves into formation mechanisms using unique isotope instruments, including one exclusively at Penn State. Christopher House, professor of geosciences and co-author, explained that isotope ratios provide a measurable tag linking molecules to specific reservoirs or processes, offering clues to an older history for atoms within a molecule.</p><p>Previously, the team studied glycine, the simplest amino acid, suggesting its formation in a harsh, cold environment, contrary to the warm, wet conditions often assumed for life's building blocks. This time, they focused on beta-alanine, an abundant amino acid in space samples, not among the 20 protein-forming amino acids but capable of forming peptides. Katherine Freeman, principal investigator, noted that beta-alanine helps understand the diversity and formation of these compounds, ultimately informing how space organics shaped life's unique chemistry.</p><p>The researchers developed a new technique for intramolecular isotopic analysis, looking at individual carbon isotopes within the molecule rather than just the average. Mcintosh stated that this provides a more detailed isotopic fingerprint, distinguishing between possible formation pathways. Specialized equipment, including the pico-CSIA (unique to Penn State for tiny samples) and a modified GC-Orbitrap, enabled these precise measurements. When comparing beta-alanine from Bennu and Murchison, despite similar average carbon isotope values, the intramolecular carbon distributions were &quot;completely different,&quot; revealing distinct histories for these seemingly similar molecules.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive summary of the research findings and methodology described in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-bennu-asteroid-analysis-unexpected-space.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
