       <h1>Climate Study Reveals Cloud Reflectivity's Role in Global Warming</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>University of Wyoming researchers utilizing 250 experimental configurations of the E3SMv3 climate model discovered that marine low cloud coverage changes impact global temperatures differently based on initial cloud reflectivity. The study highlights that accurately predicting future warming requires assessing both coverage shifts and current reflective capacity.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/climate-study-reveals-cloud-reflectivitys-role-in-global-war.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Simulations revealed persistent challenges in modeling how clouds react to winds across varying ocean temperatures. Published in Geophysical Research Letters, these findings underscore the necessity of refining cloud process approximations within Earth system models to better understand environmental feedback loops.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures the research findings and methodology while maintaining a professional tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
