     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Dung beetle diversity found in Kerala island ecosystem</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study in Kerala’s Vypin-Kadamakkudy barrier island system identified 20,927 dung beetles across 29 species, revealing unexpected biodiversity in an urbanizing area. These insects are vital ecological engineers that recycle animal waste, suppress pests, and aerate soil.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/dung-beetle-diversity-found-in-kerala-island-ecosystem.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Dung beetles are categorized as rollers, tunnellers, or dwellers based on their waste-handling methods. While some species are generalists, others like the Heliocopris dominus are specialists dependent on wild elephant dung. The male horned dung beetle can pull 1,141 times its body weight. Despite their ecological importance, most Indian species remain unassessed on the IUCN Red List, with habitat fragmentation posing a significant threat.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively captures the study's key data and the ecological context of the dung beetles.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/short-article/2026/11/natures-tiny-cleanup-crew/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
