     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Equity MF Inflows Decline 14% in October as Mid-, Smallcap Investments Cool</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Mutual Funds</span> <span>#Equity</span> <span>#SIP</span></div>
       <p>Net inflows into equity mutual fund schemes experienced a 14 percent month-on-month decline in October, primarily as investments into smallcap and midcap funds moderated after a period of significant growth. Total net inflows for the month stood at ₹32,994 crore, a reduction that occurred despite a slight decrease in redemptions. Gross investments also fell by 12 percent month-on-month to ₹65,581 crore, while redemptions decreased by 10 percent to ₹32,588 crore, according to data released by the Association of Mutual Funds in India (Amfi).</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/equity-mf-inflows-decline-14-in-october-as-mid-smallcap-inve.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Mutual fund officials and market experts attribute this decline to investors booking profits and a shift in strategy towards smallcap and midcap funds following their sharp run-up. Manish Mehta, Chief Business Officer at SBI Mutual Fund, noted, 'October numbers were lower than the previous month by about 14 per cent. A rising market may have led to investors booking profits.' Smallcap and midcap funds, which had led inflow charts in previous months, saw a significant drop, collectively garnering ₹11,301 crore in October—a 33 percent decrease from September's record high of ₹16,874 crore. These categories represented approximately 34 percent of total net equity inflows in October, down from 44 percent in September. Himanshu Srivastava, Principal, Manager Research at Morningstar Investment Research India, suggested this moderation 'could indicate some degree of caution among investors towards these segments, particularly given the elevated valuations.'</p><p>Despite this, Srivastava added that 'the continued healthy inflows suggest that investors remain constructive on their long-term growth prospects.' In a contrasting trend, large &amp; midcap funds saw their net inflows nearly double to ₹8,484 crore last month. Suranjana Borthakhur, Head of Distribution &amp; Strategic Alliances at Mirae Asset Investment Managers (India), attributed this to investor diversification, calling it a 'healthy sign.' The systematic investment plan (SIP) data also reflected the broader decline, with inflows marginally dipping to ₹32,160 crore in October from ₹32,490 crore in September. Amfi linked this to a slight drop in contributing SIP accounts and the previous month's final day falling on a weekend. Overall, supported by ₹1.1 trillion in debt fund inflows, the mutual fund industry attracted net inflows of ₹1.7 trillion in October, pushing assets under management to ₹83.39 trillion. Equity mutual funds recorded their 68th consecutive month of positive net flows, as per Venkat Chalasani, Chief Executive of Amfi.</p><p>What to watch: Investor sentiment towards midcap and smallcap funds in coming months.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a high-quality summary that captures all essential data points and expert commentary from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
