     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Europe Proposes Digital Fairness Act to Ban Dark Patterns, Protect Minors</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#EU</span> <span>#Digital Fairness Act</span> <span>#Consumer Protection</span></div>
       <p>Europe has unveiled a proposal for new consumer protection rules, the Digital Fairness Act (DFA), aimed at curbing digital practices that harm children and compel consumers to spend more. The EU Commission's proposal targets issues such as unclear pricing, addictive design features, ads directed at minors, and &quot;dark patterns&quot; like hard-to-cancel subscriptions, as reported by Engadget. Companies found in breach could face significant fines, up to six percent of their global annual revenue.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/europe-proposes-digital-fairness-act-to-ban-dark-patterns-pr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>EU Justice Chief Michael McGrath stated that the rules aim to ensure businesses compete on product merits, not on exploiting consumers or evading regulations. The act seeks to protect consumers by banning dark patterns, mandating clear and transparent online pricing for cultural and sports events, and allowing users to disable &quot;addictive design features&quot; that encourage excessive spending. It also specifically restricts unfair practices that encourage overspending by minors, including pay-to-win elements in games (loot boxes) and persistent prompts.</p><p>Additionally, the DFA addresses influencer marketing, requiring content creators to be transparent about commercial content. It also aims to tackle unfair personalized ads and prices targeting minors, and to eliminate &quot;subscription traps.&quot; The European Commission further proposed to centralize enforcement power, granting the Commission direct authority to investigate large-scale cases with major consumer impact, improve coordination with member states, and strengthen enforcement against non-EU companies.</p><p>This shift could consolidate regulatory power and streamline enforcement across Europe, potentially reducing the current fragmented approach seen in countries like France. It may also rebalance regulatory influence away from nations like Ireland, which hosts major tech giants but has faced concerns regarding the independence of its privacy regulator from UN experts.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the Engadget reporting on the Digital Fairness Act and its implications.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2313329/europe-wants-to-ban-dark-patterns-in-apps-and-loot-boxes-for-minors/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-11/europe-proposes-digital-fairness-act-to-ban-dark-patterns-pr" data-title="Europe Proposes Digital Fairness Act to Ban Dark Patterns, Protect Minors"><b>Share:</b>
