       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Himalayas Agenda Excluded from COP31 Formal Discussions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#world</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A proposal by Nepal, Bhutan, and Kyrgyzstan to include the Himalayas as a formal agenda item at the 31st UN Climate Change Conference (COP31) in Antalya has failed. The motion did not secure necessary backing from major developed nations, with reports indicating the European Union showed little interest in the initiative. </p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/himalayas-agenda-excluded-from-cop31-formal-discussions.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Nepal's Foreign Minister Shisir Khanal noted that while the agenda was not adopted, the presidency has promised to include mountain-related issues in the conference's final outcome document. Developing nations reportedly did not oppose the proposal, and organizers have scheduled a high-level research dialogue to address the urgent consequences of rising global temperatures on mountain regions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a dense and accurate summary of the COP31 agenda situation regarding the Himalayas, correctly citing the key players and outcomes.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/sci-tech/energy-and-environment/himalayas-agenda-left-off-cop31-formal-list-as-major-blocs-withhold-support-nepal-foreign-minister/article71688621.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
