     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Kerala CM Denies Misconduct in Power Purchase Controversy</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Kerala Chief Minister V.D. Satheesan has dismissed allegations that the government acted improperly regarding a power purchase proposal from GNA Energy. Satheesan stated that the Kerala State Electricity Board (KSEB) never finalized a power purchase agreement with GNA, and he denied claims that Electricity Minister Sunny Joseph was excluded from the decision-making process.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/kerala-cm-denies-misconduct-in-power-purchase-controversy.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The controversy arose after a KSEB note warned of potential power cuts, prompting the government to review supply options. While the Chief Minister defended the administrative process, opposition figures, including UDF convener Adoor Prakash, have criticized his leadership style. The government ultimately avoided the GNA deal to prevent significant financial liability.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately captures the Chief Minister's defense and the context of the power purchase controversy without adding external noise.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/keralam-power-purchase-row-cm-satheesan-plays-down-issue-dismisses-charges-that-electricity-minister-was-kept-in-the-dark/article71688584.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
