     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NASA to Welcome Expedition 74 Crew After Long-Duration ISS Mission</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NASA</span> <span>#International Space Station</span> <span>#Space Exploration</span> <span>#Astronauts</span></div>
       <p>NASA is set to welcome home the Expedition 74 crew during a public event at Space Center Houston, following their extended mission aboard the International Space Station (ISS). The event, scheduled for 6 p.m. CST on Tuesday, November 17, at the NASA Johnson Space Center’s visitor center, will feature mission highlights shared by the returning astronauts and cosmonauts, followed by an awards ceremony.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/nasa-to-welcome-expedition-74-crew-after-long-duration-iss-m.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Expedition 74 crew includes NASA astronauts Jessica Meir, Jack Hathaway, and Chris Williams; ESA (European Space Agency) astronaut Sophie Adenot; and Roscosmos cosmonauts Sergey Kud‑Sverchkov and Sergei Mikaev. Media interviews with the astronauts will be available before the event, with requests due by 5 p.m. on Monday, November 16, to jsc-newsroom@mail.nasa.gov.</p><p>Cosmonauts Williams, Kud‑Sverchkov, and Mikaev embarked on their mission aboard the Soyuz MS-28 spacecraft, launching from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan on November 27, 2025. They returned to Earth on July 26, making a parachute-assisted landing southeast of Dzhezkazgan, Kazakhstan. This trio spent 241 days in space, completing 3,856 orbits and traveling over 102 million miles. It marked the first mission for Williams and Mikaev, and the second for Kud-Sverchkov.</p><p>Astronauts Meir, Hathaway, and Adenot launched on February 13 as part of NASA’s SpaceX Crew-12 mission aboard a Dragon spacecraft from Space Launch Complex 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station in Florida. After more than seven months, or 237 days, on the space station, they safely splashed down on October 8 in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of Los Angeles. During their mission, these four crew members traveled over 100 million miles and completed more than 3,792 orbits around Earth. This was Meir's second mission, and the first for Hathaway and Adenot. Roscosmos cosmonaut Andrey Fedyaev also flew with Crew-12 but will not be available for the event.</p><p>Throughout their tenure, the Expedition 74 crew dedicated over 1,400 hours to scientific research and technology demonstrations. Key areas included advancing in-space manufacturing through metal and tissue 3D-printing technologies, and conducting human health studies to examine the effects of long-duration spaceflight. Research encompassed blood flow studies, assessments of piloting capabilities in varying gravitational conditions, and the activation of a new compact exercise system. The crew also evaluated life-support and environmental systems crucial for future exploration missions under NASA’s Artemis program.</p><p>Further scientific endeavors involved pharmaceutical research, focusing on crystallizing proteins for cancer medications, developing tumor targeting treatments, and growing stem cells for therapeutics. They managed the first set of plants studied outside curated systems, upgraded an instrument for advancing quantum technologies, oversaw visiting spacecraft, and performed essential maintenance for the orbiting laboratory. Beyond research, Expedition 74 engaged a global audience through live educational events, outreach, and sharing imagery, underscoring the importance of scientific discovery and international collaboration.</p><p>For over 26 years, the International Space Station has continuously hosted people, fostering scientific knowledge and achieving research breakthroughs unattainable on Earth. The station aids NASA in understanding and overcoming human spaceflight challenges, expanding commercial opportunities in low Earth orbit, and establishing a foundation for future long-duration missions to the Moon, as part of the Artemis program, and eventually to Mars.</p><p>What to watch: Future scientific findings from Expedition 74 research and upcoming ISS missions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively integrates all the provided biographical, operational, and mission-specific details from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-to-welcome-expedition-74-astronauts-home-at-space-center-houston/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
