     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Organelle Discovered in Deep-Sea Mollusk Facilitates Bacterial Symbiosis</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers at The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology identified a specialized organelle in the deep-sea mollusk Chaetoderma shenloong, nicknamed the Divine Dragon Glistenworm. This structure, dubbed a &quot;dracosphera,&quot; helps the mollusk maintain bacterial symbiosis to survive in oxygen-poor, hydrogen sulfide-rich cold seep sediments in the South China Sea.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/new-organelle-discovered-in-deep-sea-mollusk-facilitates-bac.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This discovery marks the first evidence of an animal evolving a dedicated organelle for chemosymbiosis. By utilizing anatomical analysis and 3D imaging, the team demonstrated how this unique biological adaptation allows the species to thrive in extreme environments, providing new insights into deep-sea ecosystem evolution and survival mechanisms.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Strong summary that effectively distills the core discovery and the researchers' hypothesis.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-deep-sea-mollusk-houses-bacteria.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
