     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NRIs Can Complete Investment KYC From Abroad Starting December 3</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NRI Investment</span> <span>#Sebi</span> <span>#Digital KYC</span> <span>#Personal Finance</span></div>
       <p>Non-resident Indians (NRIs) and other overseas investors will soon experience a significantly streamlined process for completing their investment Know Your Customer (KYC) verification in India, with new rules taking effect from December 3. A circular issued by the Securities and Exchange Board of India (Sebi) on November 4 removes the previous requirement for individual non-residents to be physically present in India for digital KYC. This change applies to NRIs, Overseas Citizens of India (OCIs), and foreign nationals residing in countries compliant with Financial Action Task Force (FATF) standards, making it much easier for them to invest in Indian shares from abroad.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/nris-can-complete-investment-kyc-from-abroad-starting-decemb.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Previously, Sebi’s KYC Master Circular of October 2023 mandated that investors undergoing digital KYC be physically present in India, effectively blocking overseas investors from using online methods. This often meant NRIs either waited until a visit to India to open an investment account or endured a cumbersome physical paperwork process. Somnath Mukherjee, Zerodha’s vice-president of corporate development, noted that e-signing was &quot;not permitted&quot; from outside India. The paper route involved printing application forms, gathering copies of PAN cards, passports, address proofs, and cancelled cheques. Each document required attestation by a notary public, an Indian embassy or consulate, or an overseas branch of an Indian bank. These attested forms then had to be couriered to India at the investor’s expense, with Zerodha founder Nithin Kamath stating the process could take &quot;2-3 weeks or even months.&quot; Mukherjee described this as &quot;the biggest drop-off point,&quot; leading many to postpone investments. Alok Dubey, CEO of PrimeWealth, added that these delays disrupted financial planning for families abroad.</p><p>The new framework allows investors to submit forms, upload scanned documents, and sign electronically without needing to travel to India. However, the practical ease will depend on how brokers implement the requirements. The process will incorporate robust safeguards during live video-based identity verification. These include random actions during the call to confirm it's live, geotagging and timestamps with tamperproof technology, and location verification to block VPNs and ensure the IP address matches the declared country. Face-liveness checks will detect impersonation attempts, and facial matching will verify the person on video against submitted identity documents. A broker official will supervise the call, with a concurrent audit of each session.</p><p>While largely digital, the process isn't entirely paperless. If a specimen signature is submitted digitally, a &quot;wet signature&quot; made in ink will be required during the video call for matching. Document attestation remains an option if original documents cannot be verified digitally. Once verification is complete, brokers must upload the KYC record to a KYC registration agency (KRA) within three working days. Furthermore, the circular aims to reduce duplication by allowing KRAs to mark verified details as &quot;validated,&quot; enabling brokers to rely on KYC already completed by another regulated entity. While this is a significant step forward, challenges remain. Mukherjee notes that opening a Non-Resident External (NRE) or Non-Resident Ordinary (NRO) bank account is &quot;still not a digital process, so that remains slow.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: How quickly brokers integrate the new digital KYC process.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A comprehensive and well-structured report that effectively covers the new Sebi regulations, the historical context, and the practical implications for investors.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/nris-can-complete-investment-kyc-from-abroad-starting-dec-3-what-changes-126111101128_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
