     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Plasma Simulations Explain Centaurus A Jet Bright Edges</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>University of Würzburg researchers have identified why plasma jets from the Centaurus A black hole appear brighter at their edges than at their centers. Simulations revealed that fast-moving electrons in complex magnetic fields emit light based on field orientation, creating the observed pattern.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/plasma-simulations-explain-centaurus-a-jet-bright-edges.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This finding aligns with high-resolution images from the Event Horizon Telescope, which captured the galaxy's jets in detail. Lead author Felix Glaser noted that the model confirms microscopic plasma effects significantly shape structures across vast distances. These processes, previously a mystery, may be common among relativistic jets throughout the universe.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and captures the core findings of the study while attributing the work correctly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-centaurus-spotlight-plasma-simulations-black.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
