     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Roku Exits Smart Home Market to Refocus on Core Streaming Platform</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Roku</span> <span>#Smart Home</span> <span>#Streaming</span> <span>#Tech Industry</span></div>
       <p>Roku has confirmed its strategic decision to discontinue sales of smart home devices, opting instead to sharpen its focus on its stated &quot;core mission: being the top streaming platform.&quot; This significant move, announced days after Fox completed its $22 billion takeover of the company, will see Roku wind down sales of its smart home hardware. A company spokesperson told Lowpass that Roku remains committed to providing &quot;continued support and services for Roku Smart Home products&quot; despite the sales cessation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/roku-exits-smart-home-market-to-refocus-on-core-streaming-pl.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The shift is already evident in Roku's product availability, with only two smart home devices—the Roku Indoor Camera 360° and the Video Doorbell &amp; Chime—remaining in stock on the brand's web store. Other offerings, including smart light strips, bulbs, the wireless video doorbell, additional cameras, smart plugs, and Roku's home monitoring system, are now listed as out of stock. Lowpass also observed that these devices are largely unavailable at many third-party retailers, with Amazon, for instance, only offering refurbished models. This gradual pivot away from smart home products has been ongoing for several months, notably with Roku ceasing to mention such devices in its earnings reports earlier this year.</p><p>Roku initially ventured into the smart home market in 2022 by introducing rebadged versions of Wyze products, before launching its own smart home hardware last year. The decision to exit this market is primarily a business one, clearly highlighted by the company's final earnings report before joining Fox. While revenue from devices saw a 33% year-over-year increase in the third quarter, hardware losses more than doubled within the same period, escalating sharply from $22.9 million to $48.6 million. Roku attributed these increased losses largely to rising costs of memory components.</p><p>Despite the smart home exit, Roku's core streaming business and associated hardware are performing robustly. The company clarified that its device revenue is derived from its own players and TVs, rather than Roku-branded sets manufactured by other companies. Its own televisions &quot;exceeded expectations&quot; last quarter, and Roku has since expanded its offerings by releasing its first OLED sets, further solidifying its commitment to the streaming hardware sector.</p><p>What to watch: Roku's continued expansion in streaming hardware and services.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report accurately synthesizes the source material, providing the necessary context regarding the business decision and specific financial figures.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2313802/roku-will-stop-selling-smart-home-devices-and-refocus-on-streaming/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
