     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tamil Nadu Documents Historic Great Trigonometrical Survey Stations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Department of Survey and Settlement and the Tamil Nadu State Department of Archaeology have published five volumes documenting Great Trigonometrical Survey (GTS) stations across the state. These stations trace back to a survey launched by Colonel William Lambton in 1802 at St. Thomas Mount, which utilized theodolite instruments for 70 years.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/tamil-nadu-documents-historic-great-trigonometrical-survey-s.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>State officials conducted extensive field surveys to identify, map, and document these historic sites to aid modern surveying and preservation efforts. The Director of Survey and Settlement has distributed these volumes to District Collectors to ensure the long-term protection of the stations under the Survey of India's jurisdiction.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and incorporates all key figures and details provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/great-trigonometrical-survey-stations-in-tamil-nadu-documented-published-in-five-volumes/article71689260.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
