     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UN biodiversity fund struggles to secure digital sequence information payments</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A United Nations fund established in 2024 to capture profits from digital sequence information (DSI) has collected only $10,588 as of November 10, 2026. Designed to raise $1 billion annually, the fund remains largely empty due to unresolved policy disputes.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/un-biodiversity-fund-struggles-to-secure-digital-sequence-in.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>At the recent COP17 summit in Yerevan, countries failed to finalize protocols regarding which corporations should pay and how benefits should be distributed. Key decisions, including accountability for online genetic databases, were deferred to COP18 in 2028. Negotiators cited a lack of preparatory information as the primary cause for relying on interim measures rather than implementing a final framework.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the fund's status and the deferment of negotiations, though it misses the specific source name of the fund.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/11/open-genetic-data-and-unclear-rules-keep-biodiversity-fund-largely-empty/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
