     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-11 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UN Chief Guterres Hits Out at Fossil Fuel Lobby as Cop31 Faces Absent Leaders</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Environment</span> <span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#UN</span> <span>#Cop31</span></div>
       <p>The third day of the UN climate talks, Cop31, has commenced in Antalya, Turkey, marked by significant absences among leaders from major polluting nations. Despite the crucial discussions, only national leaders representing an estimated 14% of planet-heating carbon emissions are expected to attend. UN Secretary-General António Guterres, praising Turkey for the &quot;excellent organization&quot; of the conference, delivered a fervent plea for accelerated action to cut emissions, directly targeting the fossil fuel industry.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/un-chief-guterres-hits-out-at-fossil-fuel-lobby-as-cop31-fac.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Guterres declared, &quot;The case for fossil fuels is collapsing,&quot; adding, &quot;Fossil fuels must go the way of the dinosaurs. And that won’t happen on its own. The dinosaurs did not have a lobby. Fossil fuels do.&quot; He further urged nations to &quot;kick the habit – and end the fossil-fuel addiction,&quot; punctuating his message with the pun, &quot;It’s make or bake time.&quot; However, the symbolic weight of these statements is tempered by the sparse attendance from the world's most influential polluters. The heads of state or government from the five biggest carbon emitters—China, the US, India, Russia, and Japan—are notably absent from the two-day summit for world leaders. Among the top ten carbon emitters, only Iran and Saudi Arabia are represented by their national leaders.</p><p>Representation is marginally better among the top twenty polluters, with national leaders attending from host nations Turkey and Australia, along with Brazil (host of Cop30), the UK, and Kazakhstan. This analysis is based on the UN's list of high-level speakers and 2025 emissions data published in the annual global carbon budget report. While the EU, responsible for about 6% of CO2 emissions last year, is represented by Commission President Ursula von der Leyen, its inclusion would only bring the total share of carbon emissions represented by a world leader at the summit to approximately 20%. The absence of national leaders is seen as more than just a scheduling conflict; their presence at climate summits is considered a powerful symbol of their commitment to the crisis, and their interventions often carry significant diplomatic weight.</p><p>Indeed, a &quot;heavyweight non-lineup&quot; of leaders from Germany, France, Italy, Denmark, Ireland, Netherlands, Canada, and Japan have chosen not to appear. This leaves the UK as the sole G7 member whose leader has made an appearance. The civil society organization Climate Action Network (CAN) underscored this concern by awarding its customary &quot;fossil of the day&quot; to &quot;the absent leaders of wealthy countries.&quot; CAN highlighted that several of these leaders are instead heading to Dublin for the EU’s first dedicated AI summit. In a press statement, CAN critically noted, &quot;The future of AI is apparently worth showing up for, while the future of the planet can wait.&quot; While Cop31 is not regarded as one of the &quot;pivotal&quot; climate cycles, much remains to be resolved following the &quot;damp squib&quot; outcome of Cop30 in Belém, particularly concerning climate finance, the just transition, and trade. Despite the widespread absences, Brazil’s President Lula, riding high after a recent election win and lauded for roadmaps to end deforestation and transition from fossil fuels, is attending, as are Ursula von der Leyen and Turkey’s President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, bringing some high-level participation to the proceedings.</p><p>What to watch: Progress on climate finance and a just transition.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article accurately synthesizes the source material, providing a clear account of the Cop31 attendance issues and Guterres's statements.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/live/2026/nov/11/climate-crisis-talks-turkey-latest-news-updates" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
