     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-11 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Yale Researchers Unveil New Method to Map DNA Folding and Epigenetic Marks in Single Cells</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Genetics</span> <span>#Biotechnology</span></div>
       <p>Researchers at Yale School of Medicine have developed a novel experimental procedure, dubbed Epi-PHR, that provides an unparalleled look at the intricate relationship between DNA's three-dimensional (3D) folding and epigenetic marks within single cells. This breakthrough, published in Nature Methods, could significantly advance the understanding of how DNA structure influences disease and development, potentially leading to better treatments for genetic conditions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-11/yale-researchers-unveil-new-method-to-map-dna-folding-and-ep.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>DNA structure, encompassing both its 3D folding and chemical modifications known as epigenetic marks, dictates which genes a cell utilizes. While all cells in an organism share the same DNA, these structural elements control gene accessibility and cellular function. Siyuan &quot;Steven&quot; Wang, Ph.D., associate professor of genetics at Yale School of Medicine, explained that the epigenome and 3D genome affect &quot;essentially all genomic functions,&quot; including transcription and DNA replication, with epigenetic changes implicated in many cancers and developmental diseases.</p><p>Previous methods struggled to combine these two factors effectively; state-of-the-art techniques for mapping epigenetic marks at the single-cell level often destroyed DNA folding, while others lacked single-cell or single-gene resolution. Wang and his team addressed this by developing Epi-PHR, inspired by a 2015 Swedish technique. Their method employs two hairpin-shaped molecules, one with an inactivated fluorescent tag, designed to bind an epigenetic mark or a gene of interest. When these molecules are in proximity, the hairpin opens, and the tag glows, allowing for simultaneous mapping of both the epigenome and 3D genome.</p><p>The team successfully used Epi-PHR to profile hundreds of genetic sites and detect various epigenetic marks across single cells. When applied to a slice of the hippocampus in the brain, the procedure revealed gradients of epigenetic and 3D structures among neurons and other cells. Wang stated that Epi-PHR is now the &quot;best technique&quot; for researchers seeking to understand specific epigenetic marks at a gene level and map its 3D folding within single cells.</p><p>What to watch: Further applications of Epi-PHR in genetic research and disease understanding.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a comprehensive and accurate summary of the scientific breakthrough, incorporating the methodology and the significance as explained in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-method-dna-epigenetic-cells.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
